.TE
.PP
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>\f[R] reads only the description of each struct, so
it builds with C++23 on g++ 15, g++ 16, clang 23 and AppleClang.
With C++26 reflection (g++ 16 with \f[CR]\-std=c++26 \-freflection\f[R])
it also checks each description against the members that reflection
reads.
basics.md states how errors come back.
The reference with examples that run in the browser:
https://asmod4n.github.io/mbedded\-cbor/schema.html.
.SS 1. Write a message and read one field
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] sensor {
    std::string name;
    std::uint16_t id;
    std::vector<double> readings;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(sensor, cbor::tag{1700}, name, id, readings);

\f[B]auto\f[R] bytes = cbor::schema<sensor>::encode(sensor{\(dqt1\(dq, 7, {1.5, 2.5}});
\f[B]if\f[R] (!bytes)
    \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(bytes.error());
\f[B]auto\f[R] const id = cbor::schema<sensor>::at_path<\(dq$.id\(dq>(*bytes);                \f[I]// 7\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const second = cbor::schema<sensor>::at_path<\(dq$.readings[1]\(dq>(*bytes);   \f[I]// 2.5\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]encode\f[R] returns a
\f[CR]std::expected<std::string, cbor::error>\f[R], and
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] returns a \f[CR]std::expected\f[R] of the value of
the member.
.SS 2. Tags and keys
\f[CR]schema\f[R] reads each struct from its description.
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(type, tag, members...)\f[R] writes it at global
scope: the tag of the struct and every public member with its key.
Each struct in a message has its own tag number.
The macro writes a specialization of \f[CR]cbor::describe_members\f[R],
which \f[CR]cbor::structs\f[R] reads too (structs.md, section 3).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] gauge {
    std::uint16_t level;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(gauge, cbor::tag{1710}, level);

cbor::schema<gauge>::encode(gauge{3});
.EE
.PP
A type whose name holds a comma, such as \f[CR]pair<int, int>\f[R], goes
through an alias, because the preprocessor splits the arguments of a
macro at each comma.
clang refuses a description of a type in an anonymous namespace that no
code uses (\f[CR]\-Wunused\-const\-variable\f[R]).
.PP
A class with no default constructor is read through a constructor that
takes the described members in the order of the description.
.PP
\f[CR]CBOR_MEMBER(type, member)\f[R] takes the name of the member as the
key.
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(type, tag, names...)\f[R] writes the
description from the names, and each name is its key.
\f[CR]cbor::member{\(dqx\(dq, &type::member}\f[R] takes the text
\f[CR]x\f[R].
A key in \f[CR]schema\f[R] is a text.
.PP
A struct with no description, with \f[CR]cbor::untagged\f[R] or with a
base class, a member that the description leaves out, and two structs
with one number do not compile.
.SS 3. Read one value
\f[CR]schema<T>::at_path<Path>(bytes)\f[R] reads a number, a
\f[CR]bool\f[R], an enum, or a \f[CR]std::optional\f[R] of one.
A path names members with \f[CR].name\f[R] and elements with
\f[CR][n]\f[R].
On a \f[CR]std::array\f[R] or a \f[CR]std::vector\f[R] of
\f[CR]std::uint8_t\f[R] or \f[CR]std::byte\f[R], \f[CR][n]\f[R] reads
one byte.
\f[CR][]\f[R] takes the index from the next argument:
.IP
.EX
std::size_t const i = 1;
cbor::schema<sensor>::at_path<\(dq$.readings[]\(dq>(*bytes, i);   \f[I]// 2.5, the element i\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A path to a member that \f[CR]T\f[R] does not have does not compile.
A path to a text does not compile here: section 4 reads a text.
.SS 4. Read a text, a list or a struct
\f[CR]schema<T>::path(std::move(bytes))\f[R] opens the message and gives
an accessor.
The bytes move into the accessor, with no copy.
A \f[CR]std::shared_ptr<std::string const>\f[R] is read in place.
An accessor reads every kind of member.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const owner = std::make_shared<std::string const>(std::move(*bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const root = cbor::schema<sensor>::path(owner);
\f[B]if\f[R] (!root)
    \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(root.error());
root\->at_path<\(dq$.name\(dq>();                      \f[I]// \(dqt1\(dq, a std::string_view\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const readings = root\->view<\(dq$.readings\(dq>();
\f[B]if\f[R] (readings)
    \f[B]for\f[R] (double const r : *readings)
        use(r);                                 \f[I]// 1.5, then 2.5\f[R]
.EE
.PP
