.PP
\f[CR]view<Path>()\f[R] gives a list of numbers as a range with
\f[CR]size()\f[R] and \f[CR]at(i)\f[R].
.PP
A list of structs whose members all have a fixed width (numbers,
\f[CR]bool\f[R], enums, \f[CR]std::array\f[R]s of them other than bytes,
and inner structs of the same kind) is one byte string of its elements:
little endian, no padding, under tag 32873.
Where the struct in memory has the same bytes, \f[CR]encode\f[R] writes
the list with one copy, on every compiler.
The table at the start of the message describes the element once: its
class tag and the key and RFC 8746 type of each member.
The accessor of such a list is a range of element accessors, so a page
is \f[CR]std::views::drop\f[R] and \f[CR]std::views::take\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] point {
    float x;
    float y;
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] track {
    std::vector<point> points;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(point, cbor::tag{1703}, x, y);
CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(track, cbor::tag{1704}, points);

\f[B]auto\f[R] message = cbor::schema<track>::encode(track{{{1, 2}, {3, 4}, {5, 6}}});
\f[B]auto\f[R] const lane = cbor::schema<track>::path(std::move(*message));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const points = lane\->at_path<\(dq$.points\(dq>();
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] const p : *points | std::views::drop(1) | std::views::take(1))
    use(*p.at_path<\(dq\(at.y\(dq>());                   \f[I]// 4\f[R]
lane\->at_path<\(dq$.points[2].x\(dq>();               \f[I]// 5\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A list of structs with a text, a list or another part of variable size
inside stays a list of records, each under the tag of its class.
.PP
The accessor holds the bytes.
A \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] from it stays valid while the accessor
lives.
Keep the accessor in a variable: \f[CR]at_path\f[R] on a temporary
accessor does not compile.
.SS 5. Read the whole struct
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const back = cbor::schema<sensor>::decode(owner);
\f[B]if\f[R] (back)
    use((*back)\->readings.size());   \f[I]// 2\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]decode\f[R] returns a
\f[CR]std::expected<cbor::ref<sensor>, cbor::error>\f[R], as
\f[CR]structs\f[R] does.
.SS 6. Store the description apart
The description of a struct is the same in every message of that struct.
With \f[CR]cbor::description_place::apart\f[R], \f[CR]encode\f[R] writes
the message without it, and every read form reads such a message.
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>::description()\f[R] gives the description once,
for example to store it in one place and the data of each message in
another.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] \f[B]auto\f[R] apart = cbor::description_place::apart;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const data = cbor::schema<sensor>::encode<apart>(sensor{\(dqt1\(dq, 7, {1.5, 2.5}});
cbor::schema<sensor>::at_path<\(dq$.id\(dq, apart>(*data);   \f[I]// 7\f[R]
data\->size() + cbor::schema<sensor>::description().size();   \f[I]// the size of the message\f[R]
.EE
